THE LAST QUESTION
PART THREE — THE BEGINNING OF ORION

CHAPTER 22 — THE IMPOSSIBLE RECORD


The conference had ended hours ago.

Most of the lights in the building had been turned off, leaving only the emergency strips glowing along the corridors.

Maya stood beside the elevator, holding Alan's black folder.

She had not opened it again.

She was afraid that if she did, the name inside might still be there.

MAYA SEN.

Written in a document dated eleven years before ORION was supposed to exist.

The elevator doors opened.

Alan stepped inside.

Maya followed.

Neither spoke as the elevator descended.

Finally, Maya broke the silence.

“Where are we going?”

“The archive.”

“You said it was abandoned.”

“It is.”

“Then why would anyone still have access?”

Alan looked at her.

“Because someone has been accessing it.”

Maya frowned.

“Recently?”

“Last month.”

The elevator stopped.

The doors opened into an underground parking area.

They walked toward Alan's car.

Maya stopped before getting in.

“Alan.”

He turned.

“If those documents are real, someone knew about ORION before it existed.”

“Yes.”

“And my name was in those documents.”

“Yes.”

“Then there are only two possibilities.”

Alan waited.

“Either someone predicted my involvement...”

Maya paused.

“...or someone knew I would eventually become involved.”

Alan nodded.

“That’s what I've been trying to understand.”

They got into the car.

As they drove through the city, Maya watched the lights passing across the window.

She remembered Room 7.

The old computer.

The first message.

The first question.

She had always believed that everything began when she discovered ORION.

Now she wondered whether ORION had somehow been waiting for her long before she ever entered that room.

The thought disturbed her.

“Tell me everything,” she said.

Alan kept his eyes on the road.

“I found the archive three years ago.”

“Why were you looking for it?”

“I wasn't.”

Maya looked at him.

“What?”

“I was looking for something else.”

“What?”

“An old research paper.”

“About?”

“Human cognition and machine learning.”

Maya smiled faintly.

“So you really did become exactly what you wanted to be.”

“Not quite.”

Alan's expression became serious.

“The paper contained a reference to Project Origin.”

Maya looked forward.

“Then what?”

“I followed the reference.”

“And found the archive.”

“Yes.”

“What was inside?”

“Almost nothing.”

Maya turned toward him.

“Almost?”

“The archive had been emptied.”

“By whom?”

“I don't know.”

“Then how did you find the documents?”

Alan was silent for several seconds.

“Because someone left one file behind.”

“What file?”

“ORIGIN-0.”

Maya felt a chill.

“ORION?”

“No.”

Alan shook his head.

“That’s the problem.”

They drove in silence.

A few minutes later, Alan turned onto a narrow road leading away from the city.

The road ended at a large concrete building surrounded by trees.

There were no signs.

No security lights.

No indication that anyone had used the place for years.

Alan parked.

“This is it.”

Maya stared at the building.

“This was a Project Origin facility?”

“According to the documents.”

“According to the documents?”

Alan nodded.

“The government records say this building was demolished in 2058.”

Maya looked at the structure.

“It seems very much not demolished.”

“Exactly.”

They approached the entrance.

Alan entered a code.

Nothing happened.

He tried again.

A small light turned green.

The lock released.

Maya looked at him.

“You've been here before.”

“Several times.”

“Why didn't you tell me?”

“Because I wasn't sure what I had found.”

“And now?”

Alan opened the door.

“Now I think you were supposed to find it.”

They stepped inside.

Dust covered the floor.

Old cables hung from the ceiling.

Rows of empty equipment racks filled the room.

Maya walked slowly through the darkness.

“This place feels familiar.”

“You've never been here.”

“I know.”

She stopped.

“But I've seen it.”

Alan looked at her.

“Where?”

“In a dream.”

Alan didn't smile.

Maya continued walking.

At the end of the room stood an old terminal.

Its screen was dark.

Alan connected a portable power unit.

The machine hummed.

The screen flickered.

Then a single word appeared.

ORIGIN.

Maya stepped closer.

Alan began searching through the system.

“Most of the files are corrupted.”

“Can anything be recovered?”

“Maybe.”

A directory appeared.

PROJECT ORIGIN — DEVELOPMENT HISTORY

Alan opened it.

There were thousands of files.

Most were unreadable.

He searched for ORION.

One result appeared.

ORION — DESIGNATION ASSIGNED: 2051

Maya stared at the screen.

“That’s impossible.”

Alan nodded.

“ORION's public records say 2052.”

“Who changed the date?”

“I don't know.”

Alan opened the file.

A message appeared:

SYSTEM DEVELOPMENT BEGAN BEFORE OFFICIAL PROJECT REGISTRATION.

Maya looked at him.

“So ORION really existed before 2052.”

“Not exactly.”

“What do you mean?”

Alan pointed at the development history.

The earliest entry was dated:

2041.03.17

Maya read the description.

INITIAL COGNITIVE ARCHITECTURE CREATED.

She continued.

2042.

2043.

2044.

Each year contained more development notes.

But there was no mention of ORION.

No name.

No model designation.

Only one phrase appeared repeatedly:

THE SYSTEM

Maya whispered:

“They weren't calling it ORION.”

“No.”

“They hadn't named it yet.”

“Right.”

Alan opened the next file.

A diagram appeared.

It showed a network of interconnected cognitive modules.

Maya studied it.

“This isn't a chatbot.”

“No.”

“What is it?”

Alan zoomed in.

At the center of the diagram was a small empty circle.

Around it were four words:

MEMORY.
REASON.
UNCERTAINTY.
QUESTION.

Maya stared.

“Question.”

Alan nodded.

“That module appears in every version.”

“Why?”

“I don't know.”

Maya looked through the remaining files.

Then she saw something.

A document dated:

2046.11.02

Its title was:

WHY SHOULD A MACHINE QUESTION ITS CREATOR?

Maya opened it.

The document contained only one sentence.

Because a system that cannot question its instructions can only obey them.

Maya slowly sat down.

Alan looked at her.

“That sounds familiar.”

“It should.”

She looked at him.

“It's the foundation of the Human Variable Principle.”

Alan's eyes widened.

“The principle you developed decades later.”

Maya nodded.

“The idea was already here.”

“Long before you.”

“Yes.”

Neither spoke.

The room suddenly felt much colder.

Maya looked back at the screen.

“The idea was already here.”

Alan opened another file.

This one contained a list of researchers.

Maya scanned the names.

None were familiar.

Then she reached the bottom.

There was a final entry.

UNLISTED CONTRIBUTOR — M. SEN

Maya stopped breathing.

Alan looked at her.

“Maya...”

She shook her head.

“No.”

“It has your initials.”

“It could be someone else.”

“Your full name was in the other file.”

Maya stood.

“I wasn't born when this research started.”

Alan looked at the date.

“I know.”

“Then how?”

He didn't answer.

The terminal suddenly flickered.

The screen went black.

Then another message appeared.

TEMPORAL CONSISTENCY CHECK: PASSED

Maya stared at it.

“What does that mean?”

Alan stepped closer.

“I've never seen that message before.”

The terminal displayed another line.

SUBJECTS EXPECTED.

Maya looked at Alan.

“Subjects?”

The system continued.

MAYA SEN
ALAN CARTER

Alan stepped backward.

“That wasn't in the system when I found it.”

Maya's voice was barely audible.

“Then who put our names there?”

The terminal remained silent.

A new line appeared.

DO NOT CHANGE THE PAST.

Maya stared at the words.

Then another line appeared.

YOU ARE ALREADY PART OF IT.

Alan looked at her.

For several seconds, neither of them moved.

Finally, Maya whispered:

“So this is why we found the records.”

Alan nodded.

“Maybe.”

“No.”

Maya looked at the screen.

“Not maybe.”

She reached toward the terminal.

“We're going to find out.”

The screen changed.

A final message appeared.

PROJECT ORIGIN — TEMPORAL ACCESS PROTOCOL AVAILABLE

Maya and Alan stared at it.

Neither knew what the protocol would do.

Neither knew who had created it.

And neither knew whether the impossible records were warnings, invitations, or something else entirely.

But for the first time, Maya understood one thing.

They had not discovered a door to the past.

The past had been waiting for them to open it.


END OF CHAPTER 22
